   "JobVacancies": "01",
   "JobDescription": "<p><strong>Infrastructure &amp; Network Management</strong></p><p>· <strong>Infrastructure and Server Management</strong></p><ul><li>Plan, operate, and continuously improve the University’s data center, Windows Server environment, Active Directory, DNS, DHCP, Group Policy, file services, print servers, storage, virtualization, backup, and disaster recovery systems.</li><li>Maintain availability, capacity, performance, patching, monitoring, and lifecycle plans for critical infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Network and Campus Wi‑Fi</strong></li><li>Manage secure, resilient LAN, WAN, internet, VPN, VLAN, routing, switching, firewall, and campus-wide Wi‑Fi services across academic, administrative, residential, and public areas.</li><li>Monitor coverage, bandwidth, uptime, and user experience; maintain network diagrams, configurations, and escalation procedures.</li><li><strong>Cloud and Identity Services</strong></li><li>Administer Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace (G Suite), including user provisioning, email, collaboration, storage, licensing, security settings, single sign-on, and multi-factor authentication.</li><li>Integrate cloud services with on-premises systems while maintaining appropriate access controls and data protection.</li></ul><p> <strong>Access Control and Surveillance Systems</strong></p><ul><li>Manage and maintain the University’s electronic access control, CCTV, video management, recording, monitoring, and related security systems across campuses and facilities.</li><li>Administer user access permissions, cards, credentials, system integrations, retention settings, audit logs, and authorized access to surveillance footage in accordance with university policies and applicable privacy requirements.</li><li>Coordinate preventive maintenance, fault resolution, upgrades, incident support, documentation, and vendor performance to ensure reliable system availability and coverage.</li></ul><p><strong>2. University Systems, ERP &amp; Digital Platforms</strong></p><p>· <strong>University ERP and Academic Systems</strong></p><ul><li>Oversee the University ERP and related student information, admissions, registration, examination, finance, HR, library, learning management, and reporting systems.</li><li>Manage roles, permissions, workflows, integrations, upgrades, data quality, vendor support, and business continuity.</li><li>Work with academic and administrative departments to translate operational requirements into secure and practical solutions.</li></ul><p>· <strong>Website and Digital Channels</strong></p><ul><li>Ensure the University website, portals, domains, hosting, SSL certificates, backups, accessibility, performance, and security are properly managed.</li><li>Provide technical governance and access control for official social media accounts and coordinate with authorized communications staff; content ownership remains with the designated department.</li></ul><p><strong>Data and Integration</strong></p><ul><li>Maintain secure data exchange, reporting, retention, and recovery processes across institutional systems.</li><li>Support database administration, audit trails, and accurate management information while protecting confidential student and employee records.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Cybersecurity, Privacy, Risk &amp; Continuity</strong></p><ul><li>Develop and enforce the University’s cybersecurity policies, standards, incident-response procedures, acceptable-use rules, access controls, and security-awareness program.</li><li>Protect networks, endpoints, servers, cloud services, ERP data, laboratories, websites, email, and privileged accounts using layered controls including firewalls, endpoint protection, filtering, MFA, logging, vulnerability management, and timely patching.</li><li>Conduct risk assessments, access reviews, backup validation, restoration tests, disaster recovery exercises, and incident investigations; report material risks and incidents to university leadership.</li><li>Support compliance with applicable data-protection, records-management, licensing, audit, and higher-education requirements.</li><li>Maintain business continuity plans for critical academic and administrative services, with documented recovery priorities and responsibilities.</li></ul><p><strong>4. Computer Laboratories, End-User Services &amp; Service Management</strong></p><p>·  <strong>Computer Laboratories</strong></p><ul><li> Manage computer laboratories, classroom technology, specialized academic software, hardware standards, images, licensing, maintenance schedules, internet access, printing, and readiness for teaching and examinations.</li><li> Coordinate with faculty to forecast software and hardware requirements before each semester and ensure compliant deployment.</li></ul><p><strong>User Support</strong></p><ul><li>Deliver responsive support to students and staff through effective helpdesk, ticketing, escalation, and service-level management processes.</li><li>Oversee desktops, laptops, peripherals, audio-visual equipment, print services, email, accounts, and remote-access support.</li></ul><p><strong>Documentation and Training</strong></p><ul><li>Maintain SOPs, inventories, configuration records, knowledge articles, and user guides.</li><li>Provide staff and student orientation, cybersecurity awareness, and practical training on institutional systems.</li></ul><p><strong>5. IT Strategy, Governance, Projects &amp; Budget</strong></p><ul><li>Prepare and execute the University’s IT strategy, annual operating plan, technology roadmap, policies, standards, risk register, and budget.</li><li>Lead infrastructure upgrades, ERP improvements, cloud adoption, laboratory refreshes, migrations, security initiatives, and other IT projects from requirements through implementation and handover.</li><li>Define scope, schedule, cost, quality, risks, procurement needs, and success measures; provide clear progress reports to management.</li><li>Establish governance for architecture, change management, data ownership, project prioritization, and technology procurement.</li></ul><p><strong>6. Vendor, Contract &amp; Asset Management</strong></p><ul><li>Manage sourcing, contracts, renewals, service-level agreements, ISP relationships, software licensing, warranties, and vendor performance.</li><li>Maintain accurate hardware and software inventories and manage the full asset lifecycle from specification and acquisition through deployment, maintenance, secure disposal, and replacement planning.</li><li>Ensure purchases are technically fit for purpose, standardized where appropriate, cost-effective, supportable, secure, and compliant with university procurement requirements.</li></ul><p><strong>7. IT Team Leadership &amp; University Collaboration</strong></p><ul><li>Lead, organize, and develop the IT team; define roles, schedules, priorities, performance objectives, on-call arrangements, documentation standards, and professional-development plans.</li><li>Create a service culture that is responsive to students, faculty, and administrative departments while maintaining security and governance requirements.</li><li> Advise University leadership on technology risks, investment priorities, service performance, capacity, emerging needs, and opportunities for digital improvement.</li><li>Build effective working relationships with academic leaders, administration, finance, HR, admissions, examinations, library, facilities, communications, vendors, and external partners.</li></ul>",
